@page "/components/sidebar"

<PageTitle>Sidebar — Unpoly.Blazor.Shadcn</PageTitle>

<h1 class="doc-h1">Sidebar</h1>
<p class="doc-lede">
    Twenty-three components, and one decision that shapes all of them: the open state is a <code>sidebar_state</code> cookie, which is where shadcn keeps it too. The server reads it and the first paint is already right.
</p>

<p class="doc-note">
    The whole shell: a header that says what you are inside, groups of rows, a footer that says who
    you are, a rail along the edge and an inset for the page itself. Every example on this page is
    given a containing block of its own — a sidebar is <code>position: fixed</code> because in an
    application it IS the left edge of the window, and inside a documentation page that means it
    would cover this one.
</p>
<Example Source="SidebarExample.razor"><SidebarExample /></Example>

<h2 class="doc-h2">Composition</h2>
<p class="doc-note">
    The whole tree, which is worth reading once: nothing here is optional except the parts you do
    not use, and the nesting is what the classes key off.
</p>
<CodeBlock Language="text" Title="Composition" Class="mt-4 max-w-2xl" Code="@Composition" />

<h2 class="doc-h2">Structure</h2>
<ul class="doc-list">
    <li><code>SidebarProvider</code> — declares the widths and carries the open state.</li>
    <li><code>Sidebar</code> — the collapsible panel itself.</li>
    <li><code>SidebarHeader</code> — sticky at the top: branding, a title, a workspace switcher.</li>
    <li><code>SidebarFooter</code> — sticky at the bottom: a user menu, settings, an action.</li>
    <li><code>SidebarContent</code> — the scrollable region between the two.</li>
    <li><code>SidebarGroup</code> — a labelled section, with an optional action beside the label.</li>
    <li><code>SidebarMenu</code> / <code>SidebarMenuItem</code> — the rows, their badges, their actions and their submenus.</li>
    <li><code>SidebarRail</code> — the strip along the panel's edge that toggles it.</li>
    <li><code>SidebarInset</code> — everything that is not the panel.</li>
    <li><code>SidebarTrigger</code> — the button that toggles it.</li>
</ul>
<p class="doc-note">
    <code>SidebarProvider</code> declares the widths and the state; <code>Sidebar</code> is the
    panel; <code>SidebarInset</code> is everything that is not the panel. The panel is fixed and
    takes no space, so a spacer inside it reserves the width — that is what stops the page jumping
    when it collapses.
</p>
<CodeBlock Language="csharp" Title="MainLayout.razor" Class="mt-4 max-w-2xl" Code="@Structure" />

<h2 class="doc-h2">SidebarProvider</h2>
<p class="doc-note">
    shadcn keeps the open state in React and mirrors it into a cookie so the next page renders the
    same way. Here the cookie is the whole mechanism: the server reads it and passes
    <code>Open</code>, so the first paint is already right and there is no flash of a sidebar
    opening itself. <code>ui.js</code> writes the cookie when the trigger is used.
</p>
<CodeBlock Language="csharp" Title="Reading the cookie" Class="mt-4 max-w-2xl" Code="@Reading" />

<h2 class="doc-h2">Width</h2>
<p class="doc-note">
    One sidebar: set <code>Width</code> and <code>IconWidth</code> on the provider. Several: they
    are custom properties, so each provider can carry its own.
</p>
<CodeBlock Language="csharp" Title="Widths" Class="mt-4 max-w-2xl" Code="@Widths" />

<h2 class="doc-h2">Keyboard Shortcut</h2>
<p class="doc-note">
    <Kbd>ctrl</Kbd> + <Kbd>b</Kbd>, or <Kbd>cmd</Kbd> + <Kbd>b</Kbd> on a Mac, toggles the sidebar
    — shadcn's own shortcut. It is ignored while the focus is in a field, because a shortcut that
    eats a letter is worse than no shortcut.
</p>

